1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
For many months, more than 4 million people walked across this floor.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
It's never polished, seldom buffed, never treated in any way.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And yet after this punishment, it looks the same as the day it was installed.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This floor marks the path to a new industrial adventure,

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
opened up by the peaceful applications of atomic energy.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
It's a symbol of the growing trend to produce new, improved materials with nuclear energy.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
In this case, creating an atomic revolution in wood.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:12,000
applications of radioactive isotopes are already commonplace in many diverse industrial applications

9
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
ranging from controlling the thickness of rubber or gauging sheet plastic to

10
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:34,000
assure a proper thickness, to testing welds and metal, to assuring the proper

11
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
liquid levels and cans. Control of atomic energy is given technology a tool with

12
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
which to create new products and improve old ones, and in the doing is generating

13
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
a quiet revolution. Now the fusing of wood and plastic by irradiation into an

14
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
amazing combination substance promises to bring great benefits to the

15
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
manufacturers and the users of wood products it's an exciting story of

16
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:09,000
development from an AEC supported research to commercialization our story

17
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
of this new atoms and industry adventure began at the University of West Virginia

18
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
in 1961. The steps for making wood plastics turned out to be quite simple. Wood pieces

19
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
were placed in an airtight impregnation vessel and pressure was then reduced. A liquid chemical

20
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
called a monomer was introduced into the vessel and an overpressure was applied to force it

21
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:43,000
into the interior of the wood, filling the voids and cells. The irradiation step came

22
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
next, wherein the monomer-soaked wood was hardened or polymerized into a wood-plastic combination.

23
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The actual equipment at West Virginia looked like this, loading the wood,

24
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
the impregnation step, and irradiation which was accomplished by lowering the containers

25
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
to the bottom of the pool next to the cobalt 60 radiation source.

26
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The final product had all of the aesthetic appeal of wood,

27
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
but many of the properties were improved.

28
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
For instance, it could be sawed

29
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
or planed

30
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:54,000
or drilled or turned with conventional tools. Indeed, it was wood, but wood with its cellular

31
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:03,000
structure filled with plastic to give a unique new material. The Atomic Energy Commission undertook

32
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
a greatly expanded program to catalog the properties of wood plastic materials. Variables

33
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
were narrowed to several species of wood including pine, maple, oak, and fir. Among

34
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
the monomers used were methyl methacrylate and vinyl acetate.

35
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Some of the properties tested included compression tests.

36
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:47,000
impact strength, abrasion tests to determine wearing characteristics,

37
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,000
bending strength, and other important wood characteristics.

38
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
General results showed the following improvements of irradiated wood plastic

39
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:22,000
compared to untreated wood. Hardness, up 300 to 500 percent. Abrasion resistance, up 200 to 300 percent.

40
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Compression strength, up 100%.

41
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Toughness, up 100%.

42
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Dimensional resistance to humidity change, up 200 to 300%.

43
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
But not only were its functional characteristics enhanced, even its aesthetic qualities took on new glamour.

44
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The product could retain its natural wood grain and color, or by adding a dye to the plastic with which the wood was impregnated,

45
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
it could be colored throughout, or dyed to match the stain of any wood.

46
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
It's difficult to nail wood plastic because of its high plastic content.

47
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:28,000
This, however, can be overcome by pre-drilling or gluing.

48
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Conventional equipment gives irradiated wood plastic a hard, beautiful satin smooth finish,

49
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
which actually exists throughout the wood.

50
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
In practical terms, this is what it means.

51
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
A cigarette burn on wood produces an ugly scar, which normally would necessitate a new

52
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,000
finish.

53
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
But with this hybrid, whose finish extends throughout the entire structure,

54
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
a piece of sandpaper or steel wool is all that is required.

55
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Enough buffing to remove the scar, and underneath it, the same satiny finish.

56
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
With many properties of irradiated wood fairly well pinpointed,

57
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and while others were still being identified, the AEC undertook another phase of the program with the Lockheed Georgia Company,

58
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:21,000
which by this time had begun to undertake its own program of developing and marketing wood plastic products.

59
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,000
This effort was aimed towards converting standard wood products furnished by private companies into wood plastic

60
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
and returning the samples for evaluation by the participants.

61
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Most of these samples came to Lockheed, Georgia as blanks,

62
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
that is, semi-formed units from which the finished products were shaped.

63
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
These, for instance, are baseball bat blanks.

64
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And these are blanks for table legs.

65
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Lockheed, Georgia gave the wood the impregnation plus radiation treatment

66
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and then sent the polymerized blanks back to the companies that owned them.

67
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:16,000
They were then finished and tested to the manufacturing company's own standards and

68
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
specifications.

69
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,000
From a list of nearly 200 companies which asked to be included in the evaluation, about

70
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,000
80 were selected for participation.

71
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
The findings from these laboratory studies seemed clearly enough to open up yet other

72
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:41,000
industrial paths.

73
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
There were, of course, substantial questions to be answered before the laboratory experiments

74
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
could be converted into products to be manufactured and sold.

75
00:08:51,000 --> 00:09:02,000
it was AEC's responsibility to generate programs which would provide the answers.

76
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Further investigation showed that it might well become possible for a manufacturer to

77
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
produce the wood-plastic composite for about 80 cents a board foot.

78
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
One of the first questions was, was there a sizeable enough market in which the material

79
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
at that price could compete?

80
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:25,000
new product will be most competitive with pre-finished relatively high unit cost flooring.

81
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
A market research survey found that a market did indeed exist, one which represented many

82
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:45,000
millions of dollars of market value. For example, the manufacture of wood products that could

83
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:55,000
combine toughness with aesthetic qualities for commercial establishments such as schools

84
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:02,000
or institutions.

85
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
And even private homes where furniture cannot always be protected from a certain degree

86
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
of punishment.

87
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:13,000
It ranges from these to many other items where the requirements are for appearance, permanence

88
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:21,000
of finish, ease of fabrication, and stain resistance. And it very definitely included

89
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:27,000
flooring which must stand up under steady heavy wear. Here then is a new material with

90
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:34,000
a host of improved properties, with good working characteristics, with favorable market potential,

91
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
and one for which economics appear to be realistic. Let's take a look at some of the commercial

92
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:49,000
plans and activities currently going on.

93
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
The earliest entry into the wood-plastic field is the American Nova Wood Corporation of Lynchburg,

94
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Virginia.

95
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
One of its most important applications is institutional or commercial flooring called

96
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
gamma-par, where high-wear, low-maintenance characteristics are important, such as in

97
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
this meeting hall.

98
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,000
In early 1969, American Novawood was awarded a contract to provide over 400,000 square

99
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,000
feet of flooring for the new Kansas City International Airport.

100
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Over one and a half miles of guard rails, paneling, and other Novawood products are

101
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
also included.

102
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
Once installed, sanded, and buffed, an occasional damp mopping of Novawood is sufficient to

103
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,000
maintain its striking appearance, which often enhances the warmth and beauty of

104
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:48,000
natural wood. Other applications of high wear areas are ballroom floors,

105
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
basketball courts,

106
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
and bowling alleys.

107
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Another large volume use will be in furniture and furniture parts.

108
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
This table, now several years old, was made by the Stanley Furniture Company

109
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
from a black-dyed birch and methylmethacrylate composite.

110
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
The top is quarter-inch thick wood plastic glued to a core stock,

111
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and the edges are from solid stock.

112
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Another table was made from a maple and methyl methaquilate composite by the

113
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Craylor Manufacturing Company. It is solid wood plastic throughout. Certain

114
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
parts of furniture, such as arms, could provide a colorful and attractive appeal.

115
00:12:51,000 --> 00:13:06,000
as good picture frames. Textile spindles are now made routinely. Industrial dyes and molds

116
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:12,000
have been fabricated. Here a wood plastic dye is being made for the air cleaner on a carburetor.

117
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:23,000
From it, the final metal mold will be made and finally the end product.

118
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:46,000
all in all the uses for this new material are limited only by the user's imagination and desire

119
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
to be creative the basic versatility of wood now enhanced with new properties permits an

120
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,000
endless array of both large-scale and small-scale uses of wood plastics.

121
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Research work supported by the AEC has drawn to an orderly close, mainly because of the

122
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
strong commercialization effort now underway by several private companies.

123
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
One of the latest private companies in this new field is Numek, a subsidiary of Atlantic

124
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Richfield Company, which located its radiation facility among the rolling mountains and forests

125
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
in the heart of Pennsylvania.

126
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Pneumek is concentrating on the development and production of a parquet-type flooring called permagrain.

127
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Made from a combination of red oak and methyl methaquelate,

128
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
permagrain is offered in several colors and patterns.

129
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
This floor in Pneumek's entrance foyer has been subjected to constant severe abuse

130
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
by both employees and visitors to the facility.

131
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Yet, despite the everyday wear and tear,

132
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
the flooring still provides an attractive and impressive

133
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
example of the durability, lasting beauty,

134
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and ease of maintenance of wood plastic.

135
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Let's take a look at the early production of permagrain.

136
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Untreated wood fillets are loaded into carriers then placed into a pressure vessel where they are impregnated with monomer.

137
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Following impregnation and soaking, the boxes of fillets are then transferred to a larger watertight container for the irradiation step.

138
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:55,000
In this case, the Cobalt-60 radiation source is located at the bottom of a deep pool, where

139
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:03,000
the samples will remain until all of the liquid monomer has polymerized into solid plastic.

140
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:08,000
After several hours of radiation exposure, the wood is removed from the irradiator, ready

141
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:23,000
for transfer to the automated machinery that forms the flooring.

142
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Only the radiation energy has been used to cause this chemical reaction and there is

143
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:37,000
no trace of radioactivity in the permagrain.

144
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:41,000
peaceful application of atomic energy has attracted still another company into

145
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
the wood plastic parquet flooring field.

146
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Radiation Machine Recorporation, located in New Jersey, is constructing a very

147
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:58,000
large complex for flooring, which is scheduled for completion in early 1969.

148
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:04,000
More than three million curies of Cobalt 60 will make up the radiation source,

149
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:09,000
which when fully loaded will be the largest source of radiation ever amassed

150
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:14,000
for any commercial application. As with other irradiators, modern radiation

151
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
engineering technology will assure complete safety to the process, the

152
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:24,000
product, and to those associated with its production and use.

153
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:39,000
This is an artist's rendering of the completed facility.

154
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Complete automation of a large plant is quite feasible as an engineering study by the Vitro

155
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
Engineering Company showed.

156
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,000
The operation would work like this.

157
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:57,000
The wood planks are stacked aboard a carrier, which is then transferred to the impregnator for the addition of monomer.

158
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:14,000
Air is evacuated from the wood pores by a vacuum pump down to approximately one millimeter of mercury.

159
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Pressurization with nitrogen is started and the pressure is raised to a predetermined level.

160
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:37,000
The monomer is introduced into the carrier containing the wood.

161
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Vitro's plan envisions the use of methyl methacrylate.

162
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Excess monomer is then removed while still under nitrogen pressure.

163
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:57,000
The impregnator tank is now brought back to normal pressure.

164
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:02,000
The head on the product carrier is then sealed by water flooding,

165
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
thus maintaining a blanket of nitrogen over the material.

166
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:13,000
Drain water and excess nitrogen are removed.

167
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:18,000
The product carrier is uncoupled from the monomer and nitrogen feed lines

168
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
and removed from the impregnator tank.

169
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,000
It is then placed at the loading station adjacent to the irradiation cell

170
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
and carried by automatic conveyor through two sets of doors

171
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
which provide a gas lock between the impregnation area

172
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:45,000
and the irradiation zone.

173
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
The radiation source is radioactive cobalt-60.

174
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:59,000
The product carrier, passing through the first leg of the cruciform-shaped source,

175
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:06,000
is given a high dose rate to get the monomer through the initiation or gel stage.

176
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:14,000
the carrier is passed on each side of the source so that the radiation dose is as uniform as

177
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:19,000
possible in normal plant operation many product carriers would be moving through the various

178
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:27,000
radiation tunnels at the same time as though they were on an endless belt finished product

179
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:36,000
carriers move out of the airlock through the checkout room and then are unloaded in the

180
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:44,000
finished wood storage area prior to shipment. This plant would produce 24

181
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:50,000
million pounds of wood plastic composite a year, approximately 5 million board

182
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:59,000
feet. It is clear that wood plastics are even now competitively priced for a

183
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:03,000
number of products and applications, as evidenced by the growing industrial

184
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,000
interests in its production and use. Not only can woods be made extremely hard

185
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:14,000
and durable but also more resistant to insects, weather, and decay. This may

186
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,000
provide great advantages in construction in tropical countries where such

187
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:25,000
properties are important. The accomplishments so far have produced

188
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:30,000
many satisfactions but the full potential of wood plastic composites is

189
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:39,000
yet to be developed. Great challenges still lie ahead to perfect a technology

190
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:45,000
for veneers, other materials, as well as solid woods.

191
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:12,000
To find industrial shortcuts for production, to cut production costs, but these are exciting

192
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:17,000
challenges that are felt, truly felt, only when new industries are born.
